/*  A read-eval-print loop for Cufet, written in Cufet.

    The session is one growing program. Every line you type is appended to it, the whole thing is
    handed to `cufet`, and what comes back that the previous run did not produce is printed. So the
    bindings keep, and so does anything you do to them:

        Define total as 0.
        Increment total by 5.
        State total.            → 5

    ★ The REPL classifies nothing. It does not know a declaration from a statement, and that is
    deliberate — the moment it holds an opinion about Cufet syntax it can hold a WRONG one, and the
    whole reason it shells out is that `cufet` already holds the right one. Replaying the session
    means the REPL and the compiler cannot disagree about what your program means, because the REPL
    never forms a second view of it.

    ⚠ The cost, stated plainly: your session runs from the top on every line. Bindings and
    arithmetic replay harmlessly, but anything reaching the outside world does not — `Write` writes
    again, `run` launches again. And a session using `chance` prints differently each run, so the
    comparison against the previous output stops meaning anything.

    ⚠ A session line that reads standard input eats the REPL's own, since the child inherits it.
    Every shell-out REPL has this, and it is the one thing replaying makes worse rather than only
    repetitive.

    ⚠ A line that fails is rolled back out of the session. Keeping it would refuse every line after
    it for the same reason, and there would be no way back.

    ⚠ Errors report against the ASSEMBLED program, so "line 4" counts your session, not the line
    you just typed. The book loader solved this by mapping positions inside the front end; from out
    here the only lever would be rewriting the compiler's prose, and prose is not a position.

    A line ending in `:` opens a construct, and every line after it joins on until a BLANK line
    finishes it — not until `Done.`, because a `Done.` can be followed by `In case of failure:` or
    `Otherwise:`, and stopping at the first one would submit half a construct and have it refused.
    The trailing colon is the only guess in here, and it can only ever cost an extra blank line,
    never a swallowed one, because the blank line is what actually submits.

    A line that is not a statement is tried again as a question, so `2 + 2` answers `4` rather than
    being refused. The REPL does not judge which one you meant — it offers the compiler both and
    keeps the reading the compiler accepts, which is the same refusal to hold an opinion as above.

    The line is editable where the cursor is: Left and Right move through it, Ctrl-A and Ctrl-E go
    to its ends, Backspace takes the character before the cursor and Ctrl-U throws the line away.

    Up and Down walk back through what you have typed, when there is a terminal to read keys from
    one at a time. Without one there is no editor and no history — a pipe has nothing to recall.

    `Leave.` on a line by itself exits; so does end of input. It is the REPL's own word rather than
    Cufet, so it is only heard when nothing is half typed — inside a construct it is just a line
    somebody wrote.

    Run it interpreted. Compiling it needs POSIX, because `run` is fork + execvp — the same
    constraint the shell carries.  */

/* Somewhere to put the session before handing it over. TMPDIR is POSIX, TEMP is Windows, and /tmp
   is the answer that is still right when a program is started with neither set. */
Bind text to scratch-file:
    Define folder as the environment variable "TMPDIR" but void is "".
    If the length of folder is 0, folder becomes the environment variable "TEMP" but void is "".
    If the length of folder is 0, folder becomes "/tmp".
    Return "{folder}/cufet-repl-session.cufe".
Done.

/* What a run said that the run before it had not said yet. The old output is a prefix of the new
   one — the same program, with a line on the end — so the new part is whatever sits past where the
   old one stopped. A shorter result means the program changed rather than grew, and there is
   nothing honest to show. */
Bind text to beyond, given (the text whole, the text already):
    If the length of whole is greater than the length of already:
        Define first-new as (the length of already) + 1.
        Return the characters from first-new to the end of whole.
    Done.
    Return "".
Done.

/* A terminal is something you have one of, not something you consult — so it is pulled as the
   module it is. It lives in its own file because the shell needs the same one. */
Pull a terminal.
    /* Which compiler is going to answer. A REPL that evaluated with a different `cufet` than the
       one on the PATH would be lying about the language, so it uses that one and says which — and
       says it up front, because an install two releases behind refuses today's language line by
       line and never explains why. */
    Define which as "no cufet on the PATH".
    Try to:
        Define reported as run "cufet" with arguments ("--version").
        If the exit-code of reported is 0, which becomes (the output of reported) trimmed.
    Done.
    In case of failure:
        The which becomes "no cufet on the PATH".
    Done.
    State "Cufet REPL, evaluating with {which}. Type Leave. to go, or close the input".


    /* Prompts are for somebody typing. Piped or redirected, they would land in the output. */
    Define asking as cast terminal's interactive.

    /* Raw mode is asked for, never assumed. A pipe, a redirect, or a machine without termios all
       say no, and the answer is believed — the reader falls back to letting the terminal collect
       the line, which is exactly what it did before any of this. */
    Define editing as false.
    If asking, editing becomes cast terminal's raw-on.

    /* Everything typed, in order. It is only ever read by the editor above, so a session that
       never reaches a terminal keeps an empty one and pays nothing for it. */
    Define past as a series of text.

    Define scratch as cast scratch-file.
    Define session as "".
    Define shown as "".
    Define warned as "".
    Define pending as "".
    Define running as true.
    While running, repeat:
        Define building as false.
        If the length of pending is greater than 0, building becomes true.

        /* A different prompt while a construct is open, so it is never a mystery why Enter is
           not running anything. */
        If asking:
            If building, cast terminal's put on ("... ").
            If building is false, cast terminal's put on ("> ").
        Done.

        Define raw as cast terminal's typed on (editing, past).

        /* Void is end of input — the stream closed instead of anyone saying Leave. */
        If raw is void, running becomes false.
        If raw is not void:
            Define line as raw trimmed.

            /* Remembered whether or not it works. Recalling a line to fix it is most of what a
               history is for, and a refused line is the one you most want back. */
            If the length of line is greater than 0, insert line into past.

            If building is false and line is "Leave.":
                The running becomes false.
                The line becomes "".
            Done.

            /* What is finished and ready to run. Empty until something is. */
            Define ready as "".

            /* Mid-construct. The raw line joins rather than the trimmed one, so what gets assembled
               is indented the way it was typed. */
            If building:
                If the length of line is 0:
                    The ready becomes pending.
                    The pending becomes "".
                Done.
                If the length of line is greater than 0, the pending becomes "{pending}{raw}\n".
            Done.

            /* Nothing half typed: a trailing colon says more is coming, anything else stands alone. */
            If building is false and the length of line is greater than 0:
                If the last 1 characters of line is ":", the pending becomes "{raw}\n".
                If the last 1 characters of line is not ":", the ready becomes "{raw}\n".
            Done.

            If the length of ready is greater than 0:
                Define attempt as "{session}{ready}".

                /*  The same thing said as an answer rather than an instruction. `2 + 2` is not a
                    statement in Cufet and never will be, so typing it alone is refused — but it is
                    obviously a question, and a REPL that cannot answer a question is a text editor.

                    ★ The REPL still decides NOTHING. It does not look at the line and judge it an
                    expression; it offers the compiler both readings and keeps whichever one the
                    compiler accepts. Asking twice is what lets it stay ignorant of Cufet, which is
                    the whole reason it shells out.

                    The trailing stop comes off first because the wrap puts one back. That makes a
                    forgotten stop free on an expression — `2 + 2` and `2 + 2.` are both questions
                    with the same answer. */
                Define inner as ready trimmed.
                If the length of inner is greater than 0 and the last 1 characters of inner is ".":
                    Define without-stop as (the length of inner) - 1.
                    The inner becomes the characters from 1 to without-stop of inner.
                Done.
                Define asked as "{session}State ({inner}).\n".

                /* Writing and launching can each fail — a full disk, or no `cufet` on the PATH. Both
                   are failure values, so a bad line costs a message and not the session. */
                Try to:
                    Write attempt to the file scratch.
                    Define answered as run "cufet" with arguments (scratch).

                    Define verdict as the exit-code of answered.
                    Define said as the output of answered.
                    Define complaint as the errors of answered.
                    Define kept as attempt.

                    /*  Refused as an instruction, so try it as a question. If THAT is refused too,
                        the complaint held onto is the FIRST one — it is about the line that was
                        actually typed, where the second is about a line the REPL made up. */
                    If verdict is not 0:
                        Write asked to the file scratch.
                        Define answered-again as run "cufet" with arguments (scratch).
                        If the exit-code of answered-again is 0:
                            The verdict becomes 0.
                            The said becomes the output of answered-again.
                            The complaint becomes the errors of answered-again.
                            The kept becomes asked.
                        Done.
                    Done.

                    /* Nonzero still means it did not get through: a refusal, or a death part way.
                       Either way it does not join the session, and what the compiler said about it
                       is the whole of what there is to show. */
                    If verdict is not 0, state complaint trimmed.
                    If verdict is 0:
                        The session becomes kept.
                        Define spoke as cast beyond on (said, shown).
                        Define grumbled as cast beyond on (complaint, warned).
                        The shown becomes said.
                        The warned becomes complaint.

                        /* Warnings first, because one of them is usually why the output is not what
                           was expected. */
                        If the length of grumbled is greater than 0, state grumbled trimmed.
                        If the length of spoke is greater than 0, state spoke trimmed.
                    Done.
                Done.
                In case of failure:
                    State "cufet-repl: {the message of the failure}".
                Done.
            Done.
        Done.
    Done.

    /* The terminal is not ours to keep. */
    If editing, cast terminal's raw-off.
Done.
